     "label": "Authorisation",
     "read_at": "2026-11-16",
     "source": {
      "publisher": "Central Drugs Standard Control Organization",
      "url": "https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/opencms/en/Home/"
     },
     "value": "Under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, CDSCO is responsible for approval of drugs and clinical trials and coordinates State Drug Control Organizations; with state regulators it jointly licenses some critical categories such as blood products, IV fluids, vaccines and sera."
    }
   ],
   "title": "How a medicine is authorised"
  },
  {
   "lines": [
    {
     "label": "Scheme",
     "read_at": "2026-11-16",
     "source": {
      "publisher": "Central Drugs Standard Control Organization",
      "url": "https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadPublic_NoticesFiles/Final%20FAQs%20on%20QR%20code%2021.07.2023.pdf"
     },
     "value": "Bar Code or QR Code (Schedule H2, GSR 823(E) of 17 November 2022)"
    },
    {
     "label": "Rule",
     "read_at": "2026-11-16",
     "source": {
      "publisher": "Central Drugs Standard Control Organization",
      "url": "https://cdsco.gov.in/opencms/resources/UploadCDSCOWeb/2018/UploadPublic_NoticesFiles/Final%20FAQs%20on%20QR%20code%2021.07.2023.pdf"
     },
     "value": "From 1 August 2023, makers of formulations listed in Schedule H2 must print or affix a bar code or QR code on the primary label, or the secondary label if space is short, holding a unique product identification code, proper and generic name, brand name, maker name and address, batch number, manufacturing and expiry dates and manufacturing licence number; the manufacturer decides the unique product identification code under its own procedure."
    }
   ],
   "title": "Pack code"
